===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[work]] [[on the side]] (at a [[secondary]] [[job]]), often in the [[evening]] or during the [[night]].
#* {{quote-av|en|passage=There are three individual rear seats. They all slide, they all fold, or they can all be removed completely, so that you can '''moonlight''' as a van.|year=2004|month=July|speaker={{w|James May|James D. May}}|title=w:Top Gear (2002 TV series)|oclc=1048213736|season=4|number=7|episode=MPVs as minicabs|time=00:22:29|writers=w:Richard Porter; Paul Kerensa|network=w:BBC Two|location=United Kingdom; w:Isle of Man; Channel Islands}}
#* {{quote-av|en|author=Carl Knutson|speaker={{w|Jonathan Aris}}|episode=The Plane That Flew Too High|title={{w|Mayday (Canadian TV series)|Mayday: Air Disaster}}|date=19 August 2011|season=11|number=2|time=41:00|text=Investigators discover that Captain Ospina was forced to take a second job, '''moonlighting''' in a bar, in order to make ends meet for his family.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title={{w|The Sixth Extinction: An Unnatural History}}|year=2014|author=w:Elizabeth Kolbert|publisher=Picador|ISBN=9781250062185|passage=Believing the bones to belong to a cave bear, the quarry owner passed them on to a local schoolteacher, Johann Carl Fuhlrott, who '''moonlighted''' as a fossilist.|page=240}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} To [[engage]] in an [[activity]] other than what one is [[know]]n for.
#* {{quote-journal|en-US|author=Theodore Schleifer|title=How Biden Lost George Clooney and Hollywood|work=The New York Times|date=2024-07-11|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/07/11/us/politics/biden-george-clooney-hollywood.html|issn=0362-4331|author2=Jacob Bernstein|author3=Reid J. Epstein|passage=Mr. Katzenberg, who '''moonlights''' as a top Biden official and has worked with Mr. Clooney on philanthropy for decades, reached out to him to see if there was an off-ramp, according to three people familiar with the matter.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension|of an|inanimate object}} To perform a [[secondary]] [[function]] [[substantially]] [[different]] from its [[supposed]] [[primary]] function, as in {{w|protein moonlighting}}.
# {{lb|en|British|dated}} To carry out [[undeclared work]].

====Usage notes====
In American English, to moonlight is simply to work at secondary employment;<ref>Mish, Drederick C. (ed.). 1995. ''Merriam-Webster's Collegiate Dictionary.'' 10th ed. Springfield, MA: Merriam-Webster.</ref> in British English, it used to imply working secretly (i.e. not paying tax on the extra money earned), but more recent editions of some UK dictionaries no longer differentiate between the US and UK meaning; in both, legality of ''moonlighting'' is thus qualified with adjectives.<ref>Treffry, Diana (ed.). 1999. ''Collins Paperback English Dictionary.'' 4th ed. Glasgow: HarperCollins.</ref> <!--http://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/british/moonlight -->

